================================================================================
F-1 — RETRACTED. Round 1's blocking finding was a false positive.
================================================================================
Round 1 argued that dirty:f07df630 "could not have been produced by any run of
`bisellium red` at that HEAD", because f07df630 = sourceTreeHash(16a4415,
["studio",".bisellium"]) — the manifest's source_excludes: [examples/] missing
from the exclusion set. That inference was wrong, and wrong in a way this opus
of all opera makes inevitable.

`runRed`'s hashRepo (packages/commands/src/lifecycle.ts:490-501) builds
excludeDirs = [<studio rel>, ".bisellium", ...manifest.source_excludes] and
hands them to sourceTreeHash, which maps every entry through
normalizeExclude before isUnderExcluded compares them. With the `return dir;`
stub applied, normalizeExclude("examples/") returns "examples/" with its
trailing slash intact, and isUnderExcluded then tests `p === "examples/"` or
`p.startsWith("examples//")` — which no ls-tree path can satisfy. "studio" and
".bisellium" carry no trailing slash, so they still exclude. The exclusion set
therefore degrades to exactly ["studio", ".bisellium"] — f07df630 at 16a4415,
430279bf at b4f86b6.

The CLI hashes with the worktree's own live sourceTree.ts (node_modules/
@bisellium/shim is a workspace symlink to packages/shim, and bin/bisellium.mjs
runs packages/cli/src/main.ts through tsx), so recording behaviour 1's red
with the stub in place makes the recorder compute its own certificate with the
broken function. Round 1's synthetic-tree checks all used the FIXED shim, so
they could never land on f07df630 and concluded no run could.

Reproduced end to end, with the real tool, twice:

  $ git worktree add --detach $TMPDIR/w037r2-rep/wt2 16a4415      # round 1's HEAD
  $ <node_modules wired so @bisellium/* resolve to that worktree's packages>
  $ readlink -f node_modules/@bisellium/shim
  /tmp/claude-1000/w037r2-rep/wt2/packages/shim
  # with the stub applied uncommitted:
  $ BISELLIUM_SELLA=qa-lead ./node_modules/.bin/bisellium red W-037 \
      --behaviour 9 --sella builder-a --repo . --studio studio -- false
  # tree: dirty:f07df630598185d21c6cd497aaa7bcaccc912903   <- round 1's "impossible" hash
  # same worktree, stub reverted (git checkout -- packages/shim/src/sourceTree.ts):
  # tree: tree:ab98117f3635ef6548e0bc6136508bc51683c6e0

That also explains the pair round 1 could not reconcile: 02.log (dirty:ab98117f)
was recorded against HEAD's ORIGINAL regex, which normalizes correctly;
01.log (dirty:f07df630) was recorded against the stub, which does not. Same
HEAD, two hashes, both honest — the difference is the function under test.

