================================================================================
ADVISORIES FOR THE RETROSPECTIO
================================================================================
A-1 — a red's `# tree:` certificate is computed BY the code under test.
When an opus stubs anything on the sourceTreeHash/normalizeExclude path,
`bisellium red`'s own header is computed with the stub, so the header is
honest but not comparable to any hash computed with working code. Instance or
class: ONE instance in the whole repo. Searched — (a) every commit touching
packages/shim/src/sourceTree.ts on any ref: exactly two, 5dbf963 (introduced
it) and a888028 (this opus); (b) the `# tree:` header of all 112 red logs
under studio/ci/reds/ against the stub-shaped hashes: only W-037/01.log.
Nothing to fix here; the remedy is a rule or a lesson, not an edit. Cheapest
detection: a red whose header hash is reproducible from no commit under the
manifest's exclusion set BUT is reproducible with source_excludes dropped is
a self-reference, not a forgery — round 1 had all the evidence and read it
the other way round.

A-2 — 01.log is now a reconstruction, not the original red.
Original: # at: 2026-09-20T06:47:38.361Z, recorded 06:47:38Z, i.e. BEFORE the
implementation commit a888028 (committed 14:51:13+08:00 = 06:51:13Z) — it
proved test-first order on its own face. The re-recorded one is
07:05:24.898Z, after a888028 and after round 1's verdict (07:03:11Z), taken at
b4f86b6 by re-applying the stub over fixed source. It is a true run, honestly
timestamped, and the pre-fix original survives in history (`git show
a888028:studio/ci/reds/W-037/01.log`), but test-first order is now provable
only from git, not from the log. Cause: this censor's round-1 remedy
instruction. Cost of the round: one builder round and two censor rounds spent
on a non-defect.

